--TEST--
A `tainted` or `secret` value written through a `property<T>` is `E0401` where one property of `T` cannot hold it, even though the union of `T`'s properties can, while a plain value through the same key compiles — `rule:types/property-key-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Profile {
    public string $city = "London";
    public tainted string $note = "";
}

property<Profile> $key = "city" as property<Profile>;
Profile $profile = new Profile();

// A plain value fits every property of `Profile`, so these writes compile.
$profile->$key = "Paris";
$profile->$key .= "!";

// The key names `city`, which is a plain `string`. The `tainted` qualifier
// does not exist at run time, so these writes do not compile.
array<tainted string> $words = Core\Cli::arguments();
tainted string $value = $words[0];
$profile->$key = $value;
$profile->$key .= $value;

// The same check applies to a `secret` value.
class Vault {
    public string $label = "";
    public secret string $token = "";
}

property<Vault> $field = "label" as property<Vault>;
Vault $vault = new Vault();
secret string $pin = "1234" as secret string;
$vault->$field = $pin;
// covers: lang:types/property-keys
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `secret string`
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
